7. Observe the data transformation steps and the generated MD5 hash value.
8. Change the input message slightly and execute the experiment again.
9. Compare the generated hash values and note the differences.
10. Record the observations and results.

5. Observation

The MD5 algorithm converts the input message into a fixed-length hash value of 128 bits, represented by 32 hexadecimal characters.

Changing even a single character in the input generally results in a different hash value.

6. Result

The data transformation process of the MD5 hashing algorithm was studied. It was observed that MD5 generates a fixed-length 128-bit hash digest regardless of the input message length.

7. Conclusion

The experiment demonstrates the working principle of the MD5 hashing algorithm and the effect of input changes on the generated hash value. However, MD5 is considered cryptographically insecure and should not be used for modern security-sensitive applications.
